  <div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="C17 - Caption (copy/paste)">
    <div class="caption-flag">Caption - not for posting</div>
    <div class="caption-body">Sensory tolerance does grow over time, but the brain builds it gradually. You cannot force it overnight.<br><br>The nervous system adapts to input through safe, repeated, gradual exposure at the child's own pace. That is how the brain slowly learns an input is not a threat. Flooding a sensitive child with the very thing that overwhelms them usually does the opposite. It raises the alarm and teaches the brain that the input really is dangerous.<br><br>What actually expands their window is small, supported steps. Forcing it teaches the brain fear. Supporting it builds genuine capacity. So safety comes first, always, and then the gentle stretch. Push too hard and you set the process back. Go gently and the brain comes along. Safety first, then stretch.</div>
